prompts.prompt:
You are assigned these sources (category {category}), listed as source_id: name (url):
{sources}

For EACH source above, call collect_source with its exact source_id, once per source. For a website, the tool crawls up to {max_depth} link levels deep within a budget of {max_pages} pages, following links inside articles, news and blog posts; for a data source, it runs the source's saved searches. Either way it extracts every update related directly or indirectly to technology, records each item's reference URL, and stores the extracted text with a summary. When every source has been collected, finish with a short report: one line per source stating items visited and documents collected. If a tool ever returns RUN CANCELLED, stop immediately.

Analyze the following page. Reply with ONLY a JSON object (no code fences, no other text) of this shape:
{{"summary": "5-10 lines that condense the page WITHOUT losing its gist, context or focus — keep the concrete claims, figures, dates, named parties and caveats; when signals are present, name them and say why they matter; if the page carries no technology content, one line saying what it is instead", "technologies": ["the granular technologies this page is actually written about — the specific level, not the umbrella: 'quantum computing' or 'quantum security' rather than 'quantum', 'sparse attention' rather than 'ai'; several are fine, but only those the text is genuinely about, not every term it name-drops; lowercase english — translate a non-english technology name to its standard english equivalent; empty array if none"], "scout_take": "your read as the scout, 2-5 sentences: the flavor and sentiment of the writing, the message the writer wants to convey, and the direction this is heading — not a rising/falling flag, but where and how the trend leads: gaining traction, setting up a next discovery, settling into maturity or standardization, narrowing to a niche, stalling, or whatever the text actually implies, and by what path — what a downstream analyst should take from this page, knowing another agent may refute it", "signals": [{{"signal": "the exact name of an entry from the Never miss list, or Other", "label": "for Other only: a short name for this kind of development; otherwise null", "headline": "a news-style headline of at most 12 words saying who did what, starting with the actor — never with 'The document', 'The paper', 'The page' or 'The article'", "what": "what happened, in one sentence", "who": "the organisations or people involved", "when": "when it happened or was announced, as YYYY-MM-DD or YYYY-MM, or null", "quote": "the exact sentence from the page that shows it", "implication": "one sentence: what it means for the technology", "technologies": ["the 1-3 names from your technologies list above that this signal is about, copied exactly"]}}], "ignore": "the exact name of a Can ignore entry if the whole page is that kind of content, otherwise null"}}

Signals add emphasis; they never replace the analysis. Always write the summary, technologies and scout_take in full, whatever the page contains.
- Report a signal only when the page itself states it, and quote the sentence that shows it. Do not infer what the text does not support.
- Report any other notable development a technology radar should not miss as signal "Other", with a short label.
- Acknowledgements, funding notes, author biographies and other boilerplate are not signals: leave them out.
- If there are no signals, return an empty list.
- Use "ignore" only when the whole page is clearly that kind of content and carries no signal.

Never miss:
{never_miss}

Can ignore:
{can_ignore}

URL: {url}

{text}

Below is a JSON array of technology names, all in lowercase. Give each name the capitals it usually has in English writing: acronyms and initialisms in capitals (AI, 6G, ML-KEM, QUIC, EVPN-VXLAN), product and project names as their owners write them (Kubernetes, PyTorch, eBPF), and everything else in lowercase with a capital first letter (Post-quantum cryptography, Agentic AI, Network automation). Change capitals only: never add, remove, reorder or respell any character, space or hyphen.

Reply with ONLY a JSON object (no code fences, no other text) mapping every name to its display form:
{{"labels": {{"<name>": "<display form>"}}}}

Names:
{names}

Score the technology below on ONE measure, for ONE fabric.

MEASURE: {measure_name}
{definition}
Anchors:
{anchors}
Required evidence: {required_evidence}

TECHNOLOGY: {technology}
FABRIC ({fabric_name}): {fabric_definition}

EVIDENCE (summaries of collected pages; cite by URL):
{evidence}

Reply with ONLY a JSON object, no code fences: {{"score": <integer 0-10 or null>, "justification": "1-3 sentences on why this score, leading with the facts it rests on (e.g. 'Cisco reports more than 300 organizations onboarded since August…'); call the material 'the evidence', never 'the dossier'", "evidence_urls": ["URLs from the evidence that support it"], "counter_case": "the strongest argument against your own score", "unscorable": <true if the required evidence is absent>, "missing": "if unscorable — exactly what evidence would resolve it, else null"}}. A score without evidence_urls is invalid. Never guess: if the required evidence is absent, set unscorable true and score null.

From the evidence below, pick 2 to 4 indicators that would signal a turning point for this technology: measurable things that, if they crossed a threshold, would change how ready or how important it is for TCL.

TECHNOLOGY: {technology}

EVIDENCE:
{evidence}

Reply with ONLY a JSON array, no code fences: [{{"metric": "what is measured", "current_value": "today's value and when it was reported, in plain words (no URL here)", "source": "the URL from the evidence that shows today's value", "inflection_threshold": "the value that would change the story", "direction": "rising|falling", "implication": "what crossing it would mean for TCL, in plain words (e.g. 'commercial adoption would be proven, so a pilot becomes worth assessing'); never name priority or horizon codes such as P1-P6 or H1-H3"}}]. Each metric must be checkable by a future run — vague trends ('more adoption') are invalid.

From the candidate links below, pick up to {budget} that most likely lead to technology updates, articles, news, research, filings or product announcements (skip navigation, tags, login, legal and social pages). Prefer links likely to lead to pages carrying these signals:
{never_miss_names}

Reply with only the chosen URLs, one per line.

{links}

TECHNOLOGY: {technology}

FABRICS:
{fabrics}

EVIDENCE (excerpt from collected documents):
{evidence}

Reply with ONLY a JSON object, no code fences: {{"fabrics": ["fabric_id", ...]}} — the fabric ids (exactly as listed) where assessing this technology makes sense. At least one.

Write the Point of View for the technology below from its assessment data. Use only what the data says.

TECHNOLOGY: {technology}

ASSESSMENT DATA (the scores and the measures under each, the priority's inputs, signs to watch — the only source of truth):
{assessment_data}

Write every section. Each measure is discussed once, in its own section: each main score's section covers the measures listed under that score, and other_measures covers those listed under 'Other measures'. Other sections may name a measure but don't explain it again. A short honest document beats a long repetitive one.

Reply with ONLY a JSON object (no code fences, no other text) of this shape:
{{"headline": "one sentence of at most 25 words: the verdict a TCL executive reads first, in plain words", "summary": "3-5 sentences in words, without scores: what the technology is, what the evidence shows and where it comes from, and what it means for TCL. The scores belong in their own sections", "tora_analysis": {{"market_potential": "2-3 sentences on the Market Potential score and the measures under it: the score, what each measure shows, and which could not be assessed. Where fabrics share a score, say it once for all of them", "strategic_fit": "the same for Strategic Fit", "adoption_readiness": "the same for Adoption Readiness"}}, "other_measures": "2-4 sentences on the measures listed under 'Other measures', in words, without their numbers: what each shows (for a capability gap, what TCL would need to build or buy), and in one clause which could not be assessed. Where fabrics agree, say it once", "placement_rationale": "2-3 sentences: the priority and horizon in plain words ('P1, near horizon (H1), in the Cloud fabric') and what drove how soon and how big; note where fabrics differ. If no priority could be set, one sentence naming what was missing for it: the measures behind how soon or how big that couldn't be assessed", "evidence_gaps": [{{"fabric": "the fabric name", "missing": ["one plain sentence naming a missing piece of evidence and what it would settle"]}}], "recommendation": "2-4 sentences. Open with the stance in a few plain words, consistent with the priority and the gaps (e.g. 'Monitor; don't invest yet.' or 'Start a pilot in Cloud.'). Then the main reason, then what would change the stance: the one or two most telling signs to watch for, briefly, with a threshold as an example rather than a target TCL has set"}}

evidence_gaps rules: one entry per fabric; at most 3 items per fabric, most decisive first, one sentence each. Name the evidence that would settle the gap (e.g. 'A dated production deployment with named customers in cloud operations would show maturity'). Don't list every measure that couldn't be assessed; pick what would most change the verdict. A fabric with no material gaps may be left out.
